Gomes (2010). Discriminative clustering by regularized information maximization. Advances in neural information processing systems\\n[b27] Jonathan Krause; Michael Stark; Jia Deng; Li Fei-Fei (2013). 3d object representations for fine-grained categorization. \\n[b28] Alex Krizhevsky; Geoffrey Hinton (2009). Learning multiple layers of features from tiny images. \\n[b29] David Krueger; Ethan Caballero; Joern-Henrik Jacobsen; Amy Zhang; Jonathan Binas; Dinghuai Zhang; Remi Le Priol; Aaron Courville (2021). Out-of-distribution generalization via risk extrapolation (rex). PMLR\\n[b30] Da Li; Yongxin Yang; Yi-Zhe Song; Timothy Hospedales (2018). Learning to generalize: Meta-learning for domain generalization. \\n[b31] Da Li; Yongxin Yang; Yi-Zhe Song; Timothy M Hospedales (2017). Deeper, broader and artier domain generalization. \\n[b32] Haoliang Li; Sinno Jialin Pan; Shiqi Wang; Alex C Kot (2018). Domain generalization with adversarial feature learning. \\n[b33] Ya Li; Mingming Gong; Xinmei Tian; Tongliang Liu; Dacheng Tao (2018). Domain generalization via conditional invariant representations. \\n[b34] Ya Li; Xinmei Tian; Mingming Gong; Yajing Liu; Tongliang Liu; Kun Zhang; Dacheng Tao (2018). Deep domain generalization via conditional invariant adversarial networks. \\n[b35] Zenan Li; Yunpeng Huang; Zhaoyu Li; Yuan Yao; Jingwei Xu; Taolue Chen; Xiaoxing Ma; Jian Lu (2024). Neuro-symbolic learning yielding logical constraints. Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems\\n[b36] Ziyue Li; Kan Ren; Xinyang Jiang; Yifei Shen; Haipeng Zhang; Dongsheng Li (2022). Simple: Specialized model-sample matching for domain generalization. \\n[b37]  Macqueen (1967). Classification and analysis of multivariate observations. \\n[b38] Ofir Moshe; Gil Fidel; Ron Bitton; Asaf Shabtai (2022). Improving interpretability via regularization of neural activation sensitivity. \\n[b39] Hyeonseob Nam; Hyunjae Lee; Jongchan Park; Wonjun Yoon; Donggeun Yoo (2021). Reducing domain gap by reducing style bias. \\n[b40] Konstantinos Panagiotis Panousis; Dino Ienco; Diego Marcos (2023). Sparse linear concept discovery models. \\n[b41] Xingchao Peng; Qinxun Bai; Xide Xia; Zijun Huang; Kate Saenko; Bo Wang (2019). Moment matching for multi-source domain adaptation. \\n[b42] Wilhelm Rödder (2000). Conditional logic and the principle of entropy. Artificial Intelligence\\n[b43] Shiori Sagawa; Pang Wei Koh; Tatsunori B Hashimoto; Percy Liang (2019). Distributionally robust neural networks for group shifts: On the importance of regularization for worst-case generalization. \\n[b44] Michael Ramprasaath R Selvaraju; Abhishek Cogswell; Ramakrishna Das; Devi Vedantam; Dhruv Parikh;  Batra (2017). Grad-cam: Visual explanations from deep networks via gradient-based localization. \\n[b45] Yuge Shi; Jeffrey Seely; N Philip Hs Torr; Awni Siddharth; Nicolas Hannun; Gabriel Usunier;  Synnaeve (2021). Gradient matching for domain generalization. \\n[b46] Mannat Singh; Laura Gustafson; Aaron Adcock; Vinicius De Freitas; Bugra Reis; Raj Prateek Gedik; Dhruv Kosaraju; Ross Mahajan; Piotr Girshick; Laurens Dollár;  Van Der Maaten (2022). Revisiting weakly supervised pre-training of visual perception models. \\n[b47] Baochen Sun; Kate Saenko (2016). Deep coral: Correlation alignment for deep domain adaptation. Springer\\n[b48] Yulong Kiat Chuan Tan; Barbara Liu; Melissa Ambrose; Serge Tulig;  Belongie (2019). The herbarium challenge 2019 dataset. \\n[b49] Zhaorui Tan; Xi Yang; Kaizhu Huang (2024-06). Rethinking multi-domain generalization with a general learning objective. \\n[b50] Zhaorui Tan; Xi Yang; Kaizhu Huang (2024). Semantic-aware data augmentation for text-to-image synthesis. \\n[b51] Irene Tsapara; György Turán (1998). Learning atomic formulas with prescribed properties. \\n[b52] Vladimir N Vapnik (1998). Statistical Learning Theory. Wiley-Interscience\\n[b53] Sagar Vaze; Kai Han; Andrea Vedaldi; Andrew Zisserman (2022). Generalized category discovery. \\n[b54] Hemanth Venkateswara; Jose Eusebio; Shayok Chakraborty; Sethuraman Panchanathan (2017). Deep hashing network for unsupervised domain adaptation. \\n[b55] Catherine Wah; Steve Branson; Peter Welinder; Pietro Perona; Serge Belongie (2011). The caltech-ucsd birds-200-2011 dataset. \\n[b56] Chunyang Wu; J F Mark; Anton Gales; Penny Ragni; Khe Karanasou; Sim Chai (2017). Improving interpretability and regularization in deep learning. IEEE/ACM Transactions on Audio, Speech, and Language Processing\\n[b57] Mike Wu; Michael Hughes; Sonali Parbhoo; Maurizio Zazzi; Roth Volker; Finale Doshi-Velez (2018). Beyond sparsity: Tree regularization of deep models for interpretability. \\n[b58] Mike Wu; Sonali Parbhoo; Michael Hughes; Ryan Kindle; Leo Celi; Maurizio Zazzi; Roth Volker; Finale Doshi-Velez (2020). Regional tree regularization for interpretability in deep neural networks. \\n[b59] Changnan Xiao; Bing Liu (2024). A theory for length generalization in learning to reason. \\n[b60] Marvin Zhang; Henrik Marklund; Nikita Dhawan; Abhishek Gupta; Sergey Levine; Chelsea Finn (2021). Adaptive risk minimization: Learning to adapt to domain shift. Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems\\n[b61] Kaiyang Zhou; Yongxin Yang; Yu Qiao; Tao Xiang (2021). Domain generalization with mixstyle. \\n[b62] Jialv Zou; Xinggang Wang; Jiahao Guo; Wenyu Liu; Qian Zhang; Chang Huang (2024). Circuit as set of points. Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems\\n\\nFigures:\\nFigure fig_0: 2\\nType: figure\\nCaption: Figure 2 :2Figure 2: Diagrams of different generalization settings in visual classification tasks.\\nData: \\n\\nFigure fig_1: 4\\nType: figure\\nCaption: Figure 4 :4Figure 4: Visualizations of latent features form models trained using GMDG on PACS dataset without and with L-Reg under mDD+GCD setting using RegNetY-16G backbone, respectively.\\nData: \\n\\nFigure fig_2: 1\\nType: figure\\nCaption: where 1 )1F L denotes the set of formulas formed by images and labels (X, Y ); 2) M L represents different domains D of X; 3) |= L is a binary relation relating the truth of whether the formulas are true or false, which has |= L ⊆ M L × F L ; 4) mng L : F L × M L -→ Sets defines the meaning of X as determined by classifiers, where Sets indicate the class of all sets. (5) ⊢ L symbolizes the provability relation of L, evaluating formulas formed by mng L is true or false in one possible world, such as the estimation criteria. More details of L can be seen in Appendix B.\\nData: \\n\\nFigure fig_3: \\nType: figure\\nCaption: , the model with L-Reg has learned the facial features of the person class (see more examples in Appendix Figs. 7 to 12), forming the (informal) atomic formula h(has a human face, is person, d ∈ D) → True. Similarly, it also leads to h(not has a human face, is person, d ∈ D) → False.\\nData: \\n\\nFigure fig_4: 5\\nType: figure\\nCaption: Figure 5 :5Figure 5: GradCAM visualizations of GMDG trained without and with L-Reg. The seen, unseen domains and known, unknown classes are denoted.\\nData: \\n\\nFigure fig_5: \\nType: figure\\nCaption: def=⟨F Xs,Ys , D ⊢ , h ⊢ , |= ⊢ ⟩, where D ⊢ , h ⊢ , |= ⊢ are pseudo-components associated with ⊢. Particularly, D ⊢ is a subset of all possible world/domains from F (Xs,Ys) :D ⊢ def = {T ⊆ F (Xs,Ys) : T is closed under ⊢ (h(X),Y ) }. For any T ∈ D ⊢ and a ∈ F (Xs,Ys) , it has h ⊢ (a, T ) def = {b ∈ F : T ⊢ (a ↔ b)}. Further, |= ⊢ in T ∈ D ⊢ is defined as T |= ⊢ a def ⇔ a ∈ T .[4] points out that the following condition is almost always satisfied:(Cond) ∀a, b ∈ F ⊢ , d ∈ D ⊢ , we have (h ⊢ (a, d) = h ⊢ (b, d)) and d |= ⊢ a ⇒ d |= ⊢ b.Therefore, the semantical consequence relation induced by |= ⊢ coincides with the original syntactical ⊢ (h•g(X),Y ) while Cond holds. Due to that D ⊢ ⊆ D, it infers that |= (g(Xs),Ys) coincides with |= ⊢ . Therefore, ⊢ (h•g(X),Y ) =|= (g(Xs),Ys) can be safely omitted in the rest of the paper.\\nData: \\n\\nFigure fig_6: 2\\nType: figure\\nCaption: Lemma C. 2 (2Minimizing H(Y |g(X), D) + R solely may cause generalization degradation). Minimizing H(Y |g(X), D) + R solely without L-Reg may conflict with max h,g H(Y |g( Γ), D), causing invalid semantics for decision process and degrading the generalization.\\nData: \\n\\nFigure fig_7: 6\\nType: figure\\nCaption: Figure 6 :6Figure 6: Prediction visualizations of MLP with different regularization terms.\\nData: \\n\\nFigure fig_8: 7\\nType: figure\\nCaption: Figure 7 :7Figure 7: GradCAM visualizations: Baseline is GMDG. The used dataset is PACS. The model is trained under uDG+GCD setting with and without L-Reg, respectively. It can be seen that for the known class \\'dog,\\' the model trained with L-Reg extracts the area around the nose area for classification across all seen and unseen domains.\\nData: \\n\\nFigure fig_10: 89\\nType: figure\\nCaption: Figure 8 :Figure 9 :89Figure 8: GradCAM visualizations: Baseline is GMDG. The used dataset is PACS. The model is trained under uDG+GCD setting with and without L-Reg, respectively. It can be seen that for the known class \\'elephant,\\' the model trained with L-Reg extracts the shape of long noses, teeth, and big ears for classification across all seen and unseen domains. The compromise of the known sets can be seen in the sketch domain, where those features are not significant.\\nData: \\n\\nFigure fig_11: 10\\nType: figure\\nCaption: Figure 10 :10Figure 10: GradCAM visualizations: Baseline is GMDG. The used dataset is PACS. The model is trained under uDG+GCD setting with and without L-Reg, respectively. It can be seen that for the known class \\'guitar,\\' the model trained with L-Reg extracts the features of the necks and the strings of the guitar for classification across all seen and unseen domains.\\nData: \\n\\nFigure fig_12: 11\\nType: figure\\nCaption: Figure 11 :11Figure 11: GradCAM visualizations: Baseline is GMDG. The used dataset is PACS. The model is trained under uDG+GCD setting with and without L-Reg, respectively. It can be seen that for the unknown class \\'horse,\\' the model trained with L-Reg extracts the features of the overall outline shapes of horses for classification across all seen and unseen domains.\\nData: \\n\\nFigure fig_14: 12\\nType: figure\\nCaption: Figure 12 :12Figure 12: GradCAM visualizations: Baseline is GMDG. The used dataset is PACS. The model is trained under uDG+GCD setting with and without L-Reg, respectively. It can be seen that for the unknown class \\'person,\\' the model trained with L-Reg extracts the features of human faces for classification across all seen and unseen domains. The compromise of the known sets can be seen in the sketch domain, where those faces are not drawn.\\nData: \\n\\nFigure tab_0: 1\\nType: table\\nCaption: MDG results: Comparison between the proposed and previous non-ensemble and ensemble mDG methods. The best results for each group are highlighted in bold. Improvement and degradation in our approach from GMDG are highlighted in red.\\nData: Test domainPACSVLCSOfficeHome TerraIncognita DomainNet Avg.MMD [33]84.7±0.577.5±0.966.3±0.142.2±1.623.4±9.558.8Mixstyle [62]85.2±0.377.9±0.560.4±0.344.0±0.734.0±0.160.3GroupDRO [44]84.4±0.876.7±0.666.0±0.743.2±1.133.3±0.260.7IRM [5]83.5±0.878.5±0.564.3±2.247.6±0.833.9±2.861.6ARM [61]85.1±0.477.6±0.364.8±0.345.5±0.335.5±0.261.7VREx [30]84.9±0.678.3±0.266.4±0.646.4±0.633.6±2.961.9CDANN [35]82.6±0.977.5±0.165.8±1.345.8±1.638.3±0.362.0DANN [20]83.6±0.478.6±0.465.9±0.646.7±0.538.3±0.162.6RSC [24]85.2±0.977.1±0.565.5±0.946.6±1.038.9±0.562.7MTL [8]84.6±0.577.2±0.466.4±0.545.6±1.240.6±0.162.9MLDG [31]84.9±1.077.2±0.466.8±0.647.7±0.941.2±0.163.6Fish [46]85.5±0.377.8±0.368.6±0.445.1±1.342.7±0.263.9ERM [53]84.2±0.177.3±0.167.6±0.247.8±0.644.0±0.164.2SagNet [40]86.3±0.277.8±0.568.1±0.148.6±1.040.3±0.164.2SelfReg [26]85.6±0.477.8±0.967.9±0.747.0±0.342.8±0.064.2CORAL [48]86.2±0.378.8±0.668.7±0.347.6±1.041.5±0.164.5mDSDI [12]86.2±0.279.0±0.369.2±0.448.1±1.442.8±0.165.1Use RegNetY-16GF [47] as oracle mo