] Peter Dayan; Laurence F Abbott (2001). Theoretical Neuroscience. \\n[b133] Wilson Truccolo (2005). A point process framework for relating neural spiking activity to spiking history, neural ensemble, and extrinsic covariate effects. Journal of neurophysiology\\n[b134] F Zachary; Terrence J Mainen;  Sejnowski (1995). Reliability of spike timing in neocortical neurons. Science\\n[b135]  Michael R Deweese (2003). Binary spiking in auditory cortex. Journal of Neuroscience\\n[b136] H William; Charles F Calvin;  Stevens (1968). Synaptic noise and other sources of randomness in motoneuron interspike intervals. Journal of neurophysiology\\n[b137] Christina Allen; Charles F Stevens (1994). An evaluation of causes for unreliability of synaptic transmission. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences\\n[b138] Matteo Carandini (2004). Amplification of trial-to-trial response variability by neurons in visual cortex. PLoS biology\\n[b139] Alison I Weber; Jonathan W Pillow (2017). Capturing the dynamical repertoire of single neurons with generalized linear models. Neural Computation\\n[b140] A Daniel;  Butts (2016). Nonlinear computations shaping temporal processing of precortical vision. Journal of Neurophysiology\\n[b141] J Jagruti;  Pattadkal (2024). Synchrony dynamics underlie irregular neocortical spiking. bioRxiv\\n[b142] Casper Kaae; Sønderby  (). Ladder Variational Autoencoders. \\n[b143]  Curran Associates;  Inc (2016). . \\n[b144] Cina Aghamohammadi (2024). A doubly stochastic renewal framework for partitioning spiking variability. bioRxiv\\n[b145] M David;  Blei (2017). Variational inference: A review for statisticians. Journal of the American statistical Association\\n[b146] Ashish Vaswani (2017). Attention is All you Need. Curran Associates, Inc\\n[b147] Prajit Ramachandran (2018). Searching for Activation Functions. \\n[b148] Stefan Elfwing (2018). Sigmoid-weighted linear units for neural network function approximation in reinforcement learning. Neural Networks\\n[b149] P Diederik; Jimmy Kingma;  Ba (2014). Adam: A method for stochastic optimization. \\n[b150] Ilya Loshchilov; Frank Hutter (2017). SGDR: Stochastic Gradient Descent with Warm Restarts. \\n[b151] R Samuel;  Bowman (2016-08). Generating Sentences from a Continuous Space. Association for Computational Linguistics\\n[b152] Hao Fu (2019-06). Cyclical Annealing Schedule: A Simple Approach to Mitigating KL Vanishing. Association for Computational Linguistics\\n[b153] Michael Teti;  Lca-Pytorch (2023-06). . Computer Software\\n[b154] Mohamed Shakir (2020). Monte Carlo Gradient Estimation in Machine Learning. Journal of Machine Learning Research\\n\\nFigures:\\nFigure fig_0: 2\\nType: figure\\nCaption: 3Figure 2 :2Figure 2: (a) Model architecture. Colored shapes indicate learnable model parameters, including the prior firing rates, r.We color code the model\\'s inference and generative components using red and blue, respectively. The P-VAE encodes its inputs in discrete spike counts, z, significantly enhancing its biological realism. (b) \"Amortized Sparse Coding\" is a special case within the P-VAE model family: it\\'s a P-VAE with a linear decoder and an overcomplete latent space.\\nData: \\n\\nFigure fig_1: 3\\nType: figure\\nCaption: Figure 3 :3Figure 3: Relaxed Poisson distribution. Samples are drawn using Algorithm 1 for λ = 1. At non-zero temperatures, samples are non-integer, but approach the true Poisson distribution as T → 0.\\nData: \\n\\nFigure fig_2: 4\\nType: figure\\nCaption: Figure 4 :4Figure 4: Learned basis elements for various ⟨lin|lin⟩ VAEs (first two columns) and standard sparse coding models (last column). There are a total of K = 512 elements, each made of 16 ×16 = 256 pixels (i.e., Φ ∈ R 256×512 ). Features are ordered from top-left to bottom-right, in ascending order of their associated KL divergence (P-VAE, G-VAE, L-VAE), or the magnitude of posterior logits (C-VAE). The sparse coding results (LCA and ISTA) are ordered randomly.\\nData: \\n\\nFigure fig_3: 5\\nType: figure\\nCaption: Figure 5 :5Figure 5: Reconstruction performance vs. sparsity of representations. (a) Results for the VAE model family. The curves are sigmoid fit to ⟨lin|lin⟩ and ⟨conv|lin⟩ P-VAE results across varying β values (β from eq. (4)). Empty circles correspond to ⟨conv|lin⟩ architectures. (b) Amortization gap for P-VAE (blue open circle) compared to sparse coding (LCA/ISTA). Solid points show results from applying the LCA inference algorithm to P-VAE basis vectors at different sparsity levels (β LCA from eq. (1)). The purple curve is a sigmoid fit, and curves from part (a) are also included for comparison.\\nData: \\n\\nFigure fig_4: \\nType: figure\\nCaption: rδr) z e -rδr /z! r z e -r /z! = E z∼q log rδr r z e -rδr+r = E z∼q log δr z + log e -rδr+r = E z∼q z log δr -rδr + r = E z∼q z log δr -rδr + r = rδr log δr -rδr + r = r (1 -δr + δr log δr) = rf (δr),\\nData: \\n\\nFigure fig_6: 6\\nType: figure\\nCaption: Figure 6 :6Figure 6: Left, residual term f (δr) from eq. (14). Right, quadratic approximation of f from eq. (15).\\nData: \\n\\nFigure fig_7: 7\\nType: figure\\nCaption: Figure 7 :7Figure 7: Performance drop relative to the best fit. Blue circles indicate P-VAE results, red circles indicate G-VAE results, and each set of n = 5 circles corresponds to five random initializations.Using Monte Carlo samples[153] and our Poisson reparameterization trick (Algorithm 1) to estimate gradients performs comparably to using exact gradients (see eqs. (21), (23) and (24)). Table2provides a tabular summary of these results. EX, exact, MC, Monte-Carlo, ST, straight-through[107].\\nData: \\n\\nFigure fig_8: 10\\nType: figure\\nCaption: Figure 10 :10Figure10: Identifying dead neurons using a histogram-based method. We bin the KL values and determine the gap between small values and larger ones. We identify neurons with KL values lower than the identified threshold (black dashed lines) and pronounce them dead. The figure shows the distribution of KL values over all neurons (K = 512) for P-VAE, G-VAE, and L-VAE. The KL term is a single number for the C-VAE because its latent space consists of a single one-hot categorical distribution with K = 512 categories. Therefore, for the C-VAE, we use the distribution of decoder weight norms instead. These are the same models shown in Fig.4, where both encoder and decoder are linear. Table3uses this method to quantify the proportion of active neurons for VAEs across different datasets and the choice of encoder architectures.\\nData: \\n\\nFigure fig_9: 12\\nType: figure\\nCaption: Figure 12 :12Figure 12: Generated samples (left) and reconstruction performance (right). These results shown here are from models with a ⟨conv|conv⟩ architectures and latent dimensionality of K = 10.\\nData: \\n\\nFigure fig_10: \\nType: figure\\nCaption: For example (a) If the contribution is primarily a new algorithm, the paper should make it clear how to reproduce that algorithm. (b) If the contribution is primarily a new model architecture, the paper should describe the architecture clearly and fully.\\nData: \\n\\nFigure tab_0: \\nType: table\\nCaption: Algorithm 1 Reparameterized sampling (rsample) for Poisson distribution.\\nData: Input:λ ∈ R B×K >0# rate parameter; B, batch size; K, latent dimensionalityn_exp# number of exponential samples to generatetemperature# controls the sharpness of the thresholding1: procedure RSAMPLE(λ, n_exp, temperature)2:Exp ← Exponential(λ)▷ create exponential distribution3:\\n\\nFigure tab_2: 1\\nType: table\\nCaption: Models considered in this paper.\\nData: DiscretePoisson VAECategorical VAE(P-VAE)(C-VAE; [89, 98])\\n\\nFigure tab_3: 2\\nType: table\\nCaption: Reparameterized gradient estimators perform comparably to exact ones across datasets and encoder architectures (linear vs. convolutional). Exact gradients are only computable for linear decoders (see eqs. (\\nData: \\n\\nFigure tab_5: 3\\nType: table\\nCaption: Proportion of active neurons.