ansferable clean-label poisoning attacks on deep neural nets. PMLR\\n\\nFigures:\\nFigure fig_0: 1\\nType: figure\\nCaption: Figure 1 :1Figure 1: The Curve of Poisoning Effect for Various Re-training Schedules\\nData: \\n\\nFigure fig_3: 2\\nType: figure\\nCaption: Figure 2 :2Figure 2: ASR and running time for targeted attack (GM) and backdoor attack (Sleep.).\\nData: \\n\\nFigure fig_4: 3\\nType: figure\\nCaption: Figure 3 :3Figure 3: Visualization of loss landscape for Backdoor and Targeted attacks.\\nData: \\n\\nFigure fig_5: 4\\nType: figure\\nCaption: Figure 4 :4Figure 4: Attack successful rate (ASR) for different training epochs of targeted attacks\\nData: \\n\\nFigure tab_0: \\nType: table\\nCaption: Algorithm 1: Error-min+SAPA Input : Clean training set {(x i , y i )} n i=1 ; Optimization step T and M ; epochs E. Output: Sample-wise perturbation D p = {δ i } n i=1 Randomly initialize perturbation D P = {δ i } n i=1 for epoch in 1, ..., E do 1. for m in 1, ..., M do Update θ via minimizing (xi,yi)∈Dtr l f (x i + δ i ; θ), y i end 2. SAPA: Fix θ, D P , find worst direction v to maximize (x i ,y i )∈D tr l(f (xi + δi; θ + v), yi) 3. for t = 1, ..., T steps do Update δ i via gradient descent on the fixed model f (•; θ + v) end end 5.1 PERFORMANCE OF SAPA IN TARGETED ATTACKS Experiment Setup.\\nData: \\n\\nFigure tab_1: 1\\nType: table\\nCaption: Success Rate in Targeted Attacks(with standard error reported).\\nData: 16/2558/2554/255ϵ = 1%ϵ = 0.2%ϵ = 1%ϵ = 0.2%ϵ = 1%ϵ = 0.2%Bullseye3.7±1.61.1±0.41±0.30±00±00±0CIFAR10Poison-Frog Meta-Poison1.3±0.8 42.5±2.80±0 30.7±2.3 28.1±2.5 19.4±2.1 5.2±1.7 0±0 0±0 0±00±0 3.9±1.2Grad-Match72.9±3.4 2 63.4±3.8 35.4±3.1 26.8±3.4 10.3±2.6 6.4±2.8SAPA+Grad-Match80.1±3.570.8±2.7 48.4±2.8 31.5±3.1 16.7±2.4 11.2±1.5Bullseye2.1±0.30±02.6±0.71.2±0.20.5±0.10.1±0.1CIFAR100Poison-Frog Meta-Poison1.0±0.8 50.3±2.60±0 24.1±2.5 43.2±2.8 22.7±1.7 4.5±1.2 1.1±0.4 0±0 0.2±0.10±0 3.1±0.8Grad-Match90.2±3.153.6±2.5 62.1±3.5 33.4±4.4 11.3±2.5 7.4±1.9S